1
00:00:04,255 --> 00:00:10,836
[SPEAKER_01]: On February 19, 1974, I'm talking to Mickey Vernon and going to ask him some questions about his career.

2
00:00:12,377 --> 00:00:24,519
[SPEAKER_01]: I wonder if you'd start in by just briefly reviewing when you broke in, what your feelings were about playing first in the minor leagues and then making it to the big time.

3
00:00:26,439 --> 00:00:28,240
[SPEAKER_00]: Well, Doctor, I broke in in 1937 after

4
00:00:32,094 --> 00:00:34,876
[SPEAKER_00]: completing a year of Villanova College.

5
00:00:36,937 --> 00:00:46,241
[SPEAKER_00]: The team at Eastern Maryland was coached or managed by the Villanova coach, a fellow by the name of Doc Jacobs.

6
00:00:46,882 --> 00:00:54,105
[SPEAKER_00]: He was also part owner of the franchise along with a fellow by the name of Duke Landgraf from Allentown, Pennsylvania.

7
00:00:54,165 --> 00:00:58,607
[SPEAKER_00]: They are the ones that got me started in the pro ball.

8
00:00:59,188 --> 00:01:01,609
[SPEAKER_00]: I was at Villanova on a baseball scholarship

9
00:01:02,572 --> 00:01:17,084
[SPEAKER_00]: And I had come from a vocational high school and I really wasn't prepared for college, and so after the first year of college I dropped out and got into professional baseball.

10
00:01:17,224 --> 00:01:26,552
[SPEAKER_00]: And from there it just went, you know, to B-ball and then A-ball and then to the big leagues.

11
00:01:26,812 --> 00:01:30,375
[SPEAKER_01]: This Doc Jacobs was your college coach and yet the

12
00:01:32,238 --> 00:01:42,141
[SPEAKER_00]: Yes, he was a varsity baseball coach at Villanova and he also coached and managed the Class D club in the Eastern Shore League.

13
00:01:43,201 --> 00:01:44,641
[SPEAKER_00]: This was in 1937.

14
00:01:45,001 --> 00:01:52,043
[SPEAKER_00]: So I joined his club in June and played about 85 games.

15
00:01:52,083 --> 00:01:57,264
[SPEAKER_00]: They played 100 game schedules so I missed the first 15 or so games of that season.

16
00:02:00,578 --> 00:02:14,307
[SPEAKER_00]: recall any records back in that year, they had a team at Salisbury, Maryland that had 25 games taken away from them on account of a couple of ineligible players.

17
00:02:15,708 --> 00:02:22,613
[SPEAKER_00]: And on the last day of the season, Labor Day, we played the Salisbury team a doubleheader and they beat us a doubleheader and won the pennant.

18
00:02:23,253 --> 00:02:25,855
[SPEAKER_00]: So you can imagine what kind of club they had.

19
00:02:26,597 --> 00:02:30,460
[SPEAKER_00]: that they lost 25 games in the standings and still come back and won the pennant.

20
00:02:30,480 --> 00:02:32,161
[SPEAKER_01]: Why were the players ineligible?

21
00:02:32,241 --> 00:02:36,804
[SPEAKER_00]: Well, at that time you were only allowed so many classmen on a Class D club.

22
00:02:36,924 --> 00:02:41,307
[SPEAKER_00]: A classman is considered a fellow that's played a little pro ball.

23
00:02:42,808 --> 00:02:44,189
[SPEAKER_00]: Everybody else had to be rookies.

24
00:02:44,209 --> 00:02:47,991
[SPEAKER_00]: They were allowed five, I think, classmen and they had about seven or eight classmen.

25
00:02:49,352 --> 00:02:52,094
[SPEAKER_01]: Did any of those players make it big on that team you recall?

26
00:02:52,875 --> 00:02:54,276
[SPEAKER_00]: A few of them made the big legs.

27
00:02:55,332 --> 00:03:02,316
[SPEAKER_00]: There was a catcher by the name of Mike Guerra, a Cuban, that played for Philadelphia A's in Washington.

28
00:03:03,256 --> 00:03:09,480
[SPEAKER_00]: And a fellow by the name of Kamelis, a pitcher, he made it with the Cubs.

29
00:03:10,900 --> 00:03:15,683
[SPEAKER_00]: And a couple of fellas played in as high as Triple-A, but they were the only ones who made it to the big leagues.

30
00:03:17,124 --> 00:03:20,586
[SPEAKER_01]: Then from Easton to Greenville.

31
00:03:20,606 --> 00:03:23,927
[SPEAKER_00]: To Greenville, South Carolina, in the old Sally League.

32
00:03:23,947 --> 00:03:25,188
[SPEAKER_00]: It was Class B League then.

33
00:03:26,225 --> 00:03:27,246
[SPEAKER_00]: I played one year there.

34
00:03:27,987 --> 00:03:29,288
[SPEAKER_01]: Who was your manager at Greenville?

35
00:03:29,848 --> 00:03:31,369
[SPEAKER_00]: A fellow named Jiggs Donahue.

36
00:03:31,950 --> 00:03:32,611
[SPEAKER_01]: An old-timer?

37
00:03:32,771 --> 00:03:33,491
[SPEAKER_00]: Yes, he was.

38
00:03:34,912 --> 00:03:39,276
[SPEAKER_00]: And then the following year I went to Springfield, Massachusetts.

39
00:03:39,637 --> 00:03:40,898
[SPEAKER_00]: It was the Eastern League.

40
00:03:41,278 --> 00:03:45,542
[SPEAKER_01]: Were you the property of any Major League team during these minor league years?

41
00:03:46,582 --> 00:03:48,284
[SPEAKER_01]: Were these foreign teams for Washington?

42
00:03:50,226 --> 00:03:52,047
[SPEAKER_00]: That brings up an interesting story because

43
00:03:53,688 --> 00:04:06,573
[SPEAKER_00]: Doc Jacobs took me into Philadelphia to work out with the St. Louis Browns when they came to play the A's, and so they were going to have a working agreement with the St. Louis Browns, this Easton club.

44
00:04:07,713 --> 00:04:20,298
[SPEAKER_00]: So I signed with them, and this was I think before the big bonuses came into being, and they promised me X number of dollars if they picked up my option at the end of the season.

45
00:04:21,327 --> 00:04:30,650
[SPEAKER_00]: Well, Bill DeWitt, who later owned the club, came to Easton to look over the ballplayers, and he didn't pick up anyone's option.

46
00:04:31,650 --> 00:04:38,413
[SPEAKER_00]: So Doc Jacobs sold me to Joe Cambria, who had the Salisbury Club in the league.

47
00:04:38,493 --> 00:04:41,013
[SPEAKER_00]: And Cambria was a scout for Washington at the time.

48
00:04:41,714 --> 00:04:44,955
[SPEAKER_00]: So the following year, I was in the Washington organization.

49
00:04:45,435 --> 00:04:48,716
[SPEAKER_00]: But the first year, I could have been in the St. Louis Browns organization.

50
00:04:49,288 --> 00:04:53,770
[SPEAKER_00]: But my second year in pro ball, then I became property of the Washington Ball Club.

51
00:04:53,850 --> 00:04:59,734
[SPEAKER_01]: How did things go at Springfield?

52
00:04:59,754 --> 00:05:02,035
[SPEAKER_00]: Pretty good.

53
00:05:02,555 --> 00:05:04,056
[SPEAKER_00]: We had a pretty good ball club.

54
00:05:04,296 --> 00:05:09,959
[SPEAKER_00]: Jimmy Bloodworth was on the club and we were leading the league.

55
00:05:10,779 --> 00:05:16,342
[SPEAKER_00]: On July 8th, I was leading the league in hitting and I was called up to Washington.

56
00:05:17,872 --> 00:05:22,953
[SPEAKER_00]: I joined the club in Philadelphia on July 8th of 39.

57
00:05:23,353 --> 00:05:25,954
[SPEAKER_00]: And I played the rest of the season with the Washington club.

58
00:05:26,794 --> 00:05:33,555
[SPEAKER_00]: And then the following season, they sent me back to Jersey City for another year, which I think was the best thing that could have happened to me.

59
00:05:34,095 --> 00:05:42,677
[SPEAKER_00]: Because even though I was hitting good in an A league, when they took me up to Washington, I was really outclassed.

60
00:05:42,717 --> 00:05:44,457
[SPEAKER_00]: I was in over my head.

61
00:05:45,077 --> 00:05:54,825
[SPEAKER_00]: And I ended up hitting the, I was hitting 340 something at Springfield and I ended up hitting 250 something at Washington the last half of the season.

62
00:05:55,706 --> 00:06:02,291
[SPEAKER_00]: So then that following year in AAA ball, Jersey City, the International League, really, you know, helped me.

63
00:06:03,252 --> 00:06:06,134
[SPEAKER_01]: Would you have won the Eastern League title, do you think, if you had stayed there?

64
00:06:06,154 --> 00:06:07,135
[SPEAKER_01]: Were you pretty far ahead?

65
00:06:07,175 --> 00:06:09,156
[SPEAKER_00]: Well, I think I had a good chance to, yes.

66
00:06:11,585 --> 00:06:13,126
[SPEAKER_01]: Of course you were happy to get up though.

67
00:06:13,286 --> 00:06:17,670
[SPEAKER_00]: How did you feel about that July 8th?

68
00:06:18,811 --> 00:06:30,299
[SPEAKER_00]: Naturally I was glad to be going to the big leagues and to join the club in Philadelphia, which was about 15 miles from my hometown, even made it more exciting.

69
00:06:30,900 --> 00:06:38,405
[SPEAKER_00]: I didn't call anyone to tell them, I just wanted them to hear it on the radio or see it in the paper the next morning that I was in the lineup.

70
00:06:39,206 --> 00:06:40,327
[SPEAKER_01]: How did you do that first game?

71
00:06:41,560 --> 00:06:49,982
[SPEAKER_00]: Well, I joined the club, they were playing a doubleheader, and I arrived at the ballpark about the seventh inning of the first game.

72
00:06:50,882 --> 00:07:01,845
[SPEAKER_00]: I came by train from Springfield to Philadelphia, and by the time I got out to the ballpark, it was the seventh inning of the first game, and I had a tough time getting in the park.

73
00:07:02,545 --> 00:07:05,926
[SPEAKER_00]: They had to call the traveling secretary of the club to come down to the gate to

74
00:07:10,217 --> 00:07:12,518
[SPEAKER_00]: to identify me and get me in.

75
00:07:12,598 --> 00:07:18,639
[SPEAKER_00]: So I dressed and I made my debut as a pinch runner in the ninth inning of the first game.

76
00:07:19,839 --> 00:07:24,420
[SPEAKER_00]: And then the second game I played the whole game and had one for four.

77
00:07:25,180 --> 00:07:26,841
[SPEAKER_00]: I remember that.

78
00:07:26,921 --> 00:07:31,642
[SPEAKER_00]: I think the pitcher was a fellow named Beckman for the Philadelphia club.

79
00:07:34,782 --> 00:07:36,764
[SPEAKER_01]: Did you go home that night?

80
00:07:36,944 --> 00:07:38,425
[SPEAKER_00]: Yes, I did.

81
00:07:38,485 --> 00:07:41,667
[SPEAKER_00]: I went back to Marcus Hook and saw my folks.

82
00:07:41,687 --> 00:07:47,590
[SPEAKER_01]: Who was your manager at Springfield?

83
00:07:48,071 --> 00:07:49,631
[SPEAKER_01]: Was that Bloodworth?

84
00:07:49,772 --> 00:07:52,253
[SPEAKER_00]: No, Bloodworth was a player.

85
00:07:52,413 --> 00:07:55,015
[SPEAKER_00]: He went to the big leagues and played a while.

86
00:07:56,075 --> 00:08:03,460
[SPEAKER_00]: The manager was an old fellow that managed a long, long time in the minor leagues by the name of Spencer Abbott.

87
00:08:05,422 --> 00:08:09,223
[SPEAKER_00]: And he was a character.

88
00:08:09,344 --> 00:08:12,464
[SPEAKER_00]: He was, you know, in baseball, and they have a lot of those.

89
00:08:13,245 --> 00:08:21,147
[SPEAKER_00]: But at that time he had managed up until then all over the minor leagues, out in the Coast League for a few years.

90
00:08:21,787 --> 00:08:25,428
[SPEAKER_00]: They told a story about him when he managed at Portland, Oregon.

91
00:08:27,049 --> 00:08:33,191
[SPEAKER_00]: He threw up a bag of baseballs and come down and hit him on the head.

92
00:08:33,952 --> 00:08:40,197
[SPEAKER_00]: And he jumped up in the dugout and hit his head on the dugout roof and knocked himself out.

93
00:08:40,217 --> 00:08:42,458
[SPEAKER_00]: And the players just let him lay there after the game.

94
00:08:42,498 --> 00:08:46,902
[SPEAKER_00]: They didn't even take him into the clubhouse.

95
00:08:49,904 --> 00:08:53,146
[SPEAKER_01]: Who was the manager at Washington when you came up in 39?

96
00:08:53,587 --> 00:08:54,267
[SPEAKER_00]: Bucky Harris.

97
00:08:55,208 --> 00:08:55,408
[SPEAKER_01]: Yes.

98
00:08:56,629 --> 00:08:59,771
[SPEAKER_01]: OK. Now, how did they tell you you were going back to Jersey City?

99
00:09:00,832 --> 00:09:02,954
[SPEAKER_01]: Was that at the end of the season or during the winter?

100
00:09:04,132 --> 00:09:04,412
[SPEAKER_00]: No.

101
00:09:04,873 --> 00:09:17,501
[SPEAKER_00]: In the spring training of 1940, I was with the Washington Club in Orlando up until the day before they broke camp to head north.

102
00:09:18,802 --> 00:09:30,309
[SPEAKER_00]: And Mr. Griffith called me aside out the field that morning and said, we're going to send you to Jersey City on option, 24-hour recall.

103
00:09:31,411 --> 00:09:33,192
[SPEAKER_00]: He said they're training over at Winter Haven.

104
00:09:33,252 --> 00:09:36,334
[SPEAKER_00]: He said you can catch a bus and go over there and join their club.

105
00:09:38,315 --> 00:09:38,956
[SPEAKER_00]: And that was it.

106
00:09:38,996 --> 00:09:40,496
[SPEAKER_00]: That was the way they told me.

107
00:09:40,777 --> 00:09:42,418
[SPEAKER_01]: What was he like, the old fox?

108
00:09:43,278 --> 00:09:45,359
[SPEAKER_00]: Well, he was an old fox.

109
00:09:45,419 --> 00:09:47,120
[SPEAKER_00]: That's what he was.

110
00:09:48,421 --> 00:09:50,022
[SPEAKER_00]: He was a nice fellow.

111
00:09:50,282 --> 00:09:59,868
[SPEAKER_00]: He was tough to get money out of, but I guess it was because they didn't have an awful lot operating there in Washington all those years, and they didn't draw an awful lot of people.

112
00:10:00,709 --> 00:10:12,833
[SPEAKER_00]: In fact, the only year they ever, in the history of the ball club, the only year they drew over a million people was in 1946, the year after the war, when most of the ball clubs were drawing well.

113
00:10:14,133 --> 00:10:18,054
[SPEAKER_00]: And we used to draw like 400,000 a year.

114
00:10:18,814 --> 00:10:25,176
[SPEAKER_00]: And I guess he couldn't afford to pay big salaries, but he was tough to get money out of.

115
00:10:25,236 --> 00:10:27,297
[SPEAKER_00]: There was, every year,

116
00:10:29,022 --> 00:10:33,384
[SPEAKER_00]: come contract time, you know, it was... All your negotiations would be with him?

117
00:10:33,564 --> 00:10:33,844
[SPEAKER_00]: Right.

118
00:10:33,984 --> 00:10:35,965
[SPEAKER_01]: The letters from him personally?

119
00:10:35,985 --> 00:10:36,285
[SPEAKER_00]: Yes, yes.

120
00:10:36,305 --> 00:10:37,006
[SPEAKER_00]: He handled everything.

121
00:10:37,046 --> 00:10:38,346
[SPEAKER_00]: He didn't have a general manager.

122
00:10:38,586 --> 00:10:40,167
[SPEAKER_01]: Not many teams did, I guess, yet.

123
00:10:40,207 --> 00:10:40,367
[SPEAKER_01]: No.

124
00:10:40,467 --> 00:10:42,868
[SPEAKER_01]: It was certainly developing maybe about then.

125
00:10:43,288 --> 00:10:44,389
[SPEAKER_00]: Right.

126
00:10:44,429 --> 00:10:45,309
[SPEAKER_00]: He handled everything.

127
00:10:46,570 --> 00:10:48,971
[SPEAKER_00]: You know, it wasn't like the owners of today.

128
00:10:49,471 --> 00:10:57,535
[SPEAKER_00]: Most of the owners of today are fellows that go into baseball as a hobby or a sideline or a tax deduction or something or other.

129
00:10:58,633 --> 00:11:04,136
[SPEAKER_00]: Not Mr. Griffith and Mr. Mack, you know, and Mr. Yawkey and a few others.

130
00:11:04,956 --> 00:11:09,239
[SPEAKER_00]: They were in baseball because it was their livelihood and it was their life.

131
00:11:09,739 --> 00:11:15,962
[SPEAKER_01]: I always got the idea that Clark Griffith was a gentle, kind, old man.

132
00:11:16,523 --> 00:11:24,787
[SPEAKER_01]: But in studying early baseball history, he was thrown out of more games for complaining about umpire decisions than up next to McGraw, I guess.

133
00:11:24,827 --> 00:11:25,948
[SPEAKER_01]: He was the worst actor.

134
00:11:28,372 --> 00:11:36,835
[SPEAKER_00]: You know, you would get that impression if you met him in his later years that he was, like you say, and he hardly ever used any profanity.

135
00:11:37,615 --> 00:11:46,818
[SPEAKER_00]: But I've read stories and talked to old-timers, you know, that we had on the ball club, like Clyde Milan, who was one of the coaches, and Bucky Harris, who played for Mr. Griffith.

136
00:11:47,478 --> 00:11:52,539
[SPEAKER_00]: And they said he was the foulest-mouthed manager they'd ever played for, and the toughest.

137
00:11:53,340 --> 00:11:55,841
[SPEAKER_00]: But see, that's how he changed in his later years.

138
00:11:57,852 --> 00:11:58,952
[SPEAKER_00]: He was a nice man.

139
00:11:59,373 --> 00:12:05,535
[SPEAKER_00]: I'll tell you of an incident just to point out how thoughtful he was.

140
00:12:05,775 --> 00:12:06,875
[SPEAKER_00]: My wife was pregnant.

141
00:12:07,055 --> 00:12:10,337
[SPEAKER_00]: We'd been married about 11 years before we had our daughter.

142
00:12:11,177 --> 00:12:20,100
[SPEAKER_00]: And we were on our, not we, but the ball club was coming back from spring training by train.

143
00:12:20,160 --> 00:12:25,122
[SPEAKER_00]: In those days, they'd play, you know, about a week to get north, they'd play teams

144
00:12:26,375 --> 00:12:29,457
[SPEAKER_00]: on the way north in different cities and they traveled by train.

145
00:12:30,097 --> 00:12:46,067
[SPEAKER_00]: Well, Mr. Griffith was coming up to Washington on the same train that my wife was coming up to Washington on, and he walked through about six or seven train cars looking for her so she could come back and eat with he and his wife so she wouldn't have to eat alone.

146
00:12:46,847 --> 00:12:54,412
[SPEAKER_00]: And here he was around 78, 80 years old at the time, and here he was walking through six, seven Pullman cars looking for her.

147
00:12:55,846 --> 00:12:57,887
[SPEAKER_00]: Not many owners would do something like that.

148
00:13:00,247 --> 00:13:03,088
[SPEAKER_01]: How was Bucky Harris to play for?

149
00:13:04,209 --> 00:13:05,829
[SPEAKER_00]: Well, Bucky was great.

150
00:13:07,010 --> 00:13:19,814
[SPEAKER_00]: He was good with young fellas and he, I think, a little different than some of the managers of today.

151
00:13:19,954 --> 00:13:23,395
[SPEAKER_00]: He had a lot of patience

152
00:13:25,067 --> 00:13:44,020
[SPEAKER_00]: and he would let you play your game and he would let you do things that if you did them you were hanging yourself more or less, you know what I mean?

153
00:13:44,040 --> 00:13:46,421
[SPEAKER_00]: If you were doing something that you weren't supposed to be doing.

154
00:13:47,142 --> 00:13:52,025
[SPEAKER_00]: And then all of a sudden out of a clear sky he'd let you know that he knew it, you know.

155
00:13:55,110 --> 00:14:01,213
[SPEAKER_01]: Who was playing first base in 39 when you came up?

156
00:14:01,813 --> 00:14:04,794
[SPEAKER_01]: Did you share the job with them that year?

157
00:14:05,895 --> 00:14:16,399
[SPEAKER_00]: Well, at the beginning of the year, they had Jimmy Wazdell, and then they played Ozzie Bluejee, who was a third baseman most of his career.

158
00:14:16,419 --> 00:14:19,161
[SPEAKER_00]: They played him there a couple of games and a couple of other fellas.

159
00:14:23,290 --> 00:14:26,772
[SPEAKER_00]: They were unsettled at first base when they brought me up in 1939.

160
00:14:30,174 --> 00:14:34,477
[SPEAKER_00]: Then when I arrived, I played all the games the rest of the way.

161
00:14:34,497 --> 00:14:37,699
[SPEAKER_01]: In 1940, when you were down in Jersey City, did Wasdell play?

162
00:14:37,839 --> 00:14:39,620
[SPEAKER_01]: Did they have somebody else that year?

163
00:14:40,300 --> 00:14:40,581
[SPEAKER_00]: No.

164
00:14:41,121 --> 00:14:48,065
[SPEAKER_00]: No, they had gotten rid of Wasdell.

165
00:14:48,105 --> 00:14:50,146
[SPEAKER_00]: I just can't recall who played in 1940.

166
00:14:53,958 --> 00:14:56,740
[SPEAKER_00]: No, I don't remember who played first base in 40.

167
00:14:57,741 --> 00:14:59,503
[SPEAKER_01]: How were things at Jersey City that year?

168
00:14:59,523 --> 00:15:01,504
[SPEAKER_01]: How did you like playing at Roosevelt State?

169
00:15:01,524 --> 00:15:02,845
[SPEAKER_01]: Did they have big crowds there then?

170
00:15:03,186 --> 00:15:03,886
[SPEAKER_00]: Yes, they did.

171
00:15:05,347 --> 00:15:19,439
[SPEAKER_00]: Opening day, the mayor, Mayor Haig, declares half a holiday on opening day when Jersey City would open, and he sold an awful lot of tickets.

172
00:15:20,751 --> 00:15:30,139
[SPEAKER_00]: They had to announce attendance of 50-some thousand, but the place only held about 25, and it would be standing room only on opening day.

173
00:15:31,400 --> 00:15:39,406
[SPEAKER_00]: But they'd always announce the big attendance to try to claim the highest attendance in baseball.

174
00:15:40,587 --> 00:15:42,709
[SPEAKER_01]: Was there a great rivalry between Newark and Georgia City?

175
00:15:43,930 --> 00:15:48,154
[SPEAKER_00]: Yes, there was quite a rivalry because it was so close.

176
00:15:51,613 --> 00:16:08,003
[SPEAKER_00]: I can remember going over there in the bus and the kids stoning the bus as you're leaving the ballpark and hollering things at you, you know, like, hit the ramp, you're tramps, and get back up on that skyway and get back to Jersey City, you know.

177
00:16:08,783 --> 00:16:10,745
[SPEAKER_00]: But it was all

178
00:16:12,202 --> 00:16:13,783
[SPEAKER_00]: good competitive spirit, you know.

179
00:16:14,023 --> 00:16:16,725
[SPEAKER_01]: Where did you stay in Jersey City?

180
00:16:16,765 --> 00:16:20,027
[SPEAKER_01]: Did you have rooming houses or hotels with a team?

181
00:16:20,067 --> 00:16:21,468
[SPEAKER_01]: Did you stay in New York?

