357
00:33:17,413 --> 00:33:33,899
[SPEAKER_00]: We'd play, if we happened to be scheduled in Cleveland on a weekend, we'd play Friday maybe in League Park and then Saturday, or play Friday night down at the Big Ball Park and Saturday afternoon in League Park and then Sunday back at the Big Park.

358
00:33:34,639 --> 00:33:37,240
[SPEAKER_00]: And then eventually they moved all the games to the Big Park.

359
00:33:37,720 --> 00:33:44,082
[SPEAKER_00]: But I can remember, you know, switching back and forth to League Stadium and Municipal Stadium.

360
00:33:44,102 --> 00:33:45,002
[SPEAKER_01]: You like Detroit?

361
00:33:45,863 --> 00:33:46,143
[SPEAKER_00]: Yes.

362
00:33:48,075 --> 00:34:01,163
[SPEAKER_00]: Naturally, being a hitter, you kind of favor the parks that you think you've done best in, and Detroit was one of my favorite hitting parks, and New York.

363
00:34:02,284 --> 00:34:08,928
[SPEAKER_00]: Well, yes, of course I played all my home games there, but I liked Griffith Stadium for this reason.

364
00:34:09,088 --> 00:34:14,091
[SPEAKER_00]: I was kind of a spray hitter, and they had a large left field.

365
00:34:15,142 --> 00:34:24,485
[SPEAKER_00]: And this way I got a lot of base hits in the left field that maybe I wouldn't get in Boston, you know, where they had a short left field.

366
00:34:24,825 --> 00:34:31,528
[SPEAKER_01]: Did you think if you'd been in Yankee Stadium your home or outfield would have been a little better?

367
00:34:31,648 --> 00:34:32,248
[SPEAKER_00]: Oh, yes.

368
00:34:33,068 --> 00:34:33,708
[SPEAKER_00]: Or Detroit.

369
00:34:33,768 --> 00:34:35,149
[SPEAKER_01]: How about some exciting plays?

370
00:34:45,557 --> 00:34:51,821
[SPEAKER_01]: Any unusual plays?

371
00:34:52,481 --> 00:34:53,882
[SPEAKER_00]: No, I can't think of anything.

372
00:34:54,042 --> 00:34:59,685
[SPEAKER_01]: Any 1-6-1 double plays or unassisted double plays of note?

373
00:35:00,206 --> 00:35:09,171
[SPEAKER_00]: Well, the only play that I can think of would be the first triple play that I was in.

374
00:35:10,332 --> 00:35:14,174
[SPEAKER_00]: Mike Higgins was the hitter for Detroit, and I thought he was going to

375
00:35:15,372 --> 00:35:21,977
[SPEAKER_00]: and I was playing in and he hit a line shot right at me and I run over and stepped on first base and threw to second base for the triple play.

376
00:35:23,959 --> 00:35:31,445
[SPEAKER_01]: Did you have any, were you frequently able to make the play to third base on bunts, attempted sacrifices?

377
00:35:31,525 --> 00:35:34,687
[SPEAKER_00]: Yes, I made that play a few times, yes.

378
00:35:35,188 --> 00:35:38,010
[SPEAKER_00]: Ferris Fane used to make it quite often.

379
00:35:38,430 --> 00:35:40,132
[SPEAKER_00]: He was pretty good at it.

380
00:35:41,232 --> 00:35:44,315
[SPEAKER_00]: He was quick and he had a good arm and he could come over and

381
00:35:44,890 --> 00:35:50,893
[SPEAKER_00]: field the ball on the third-base side and make the play to third, which is unusual for a first baseman.

382
00:35:51,714 --> 00:35:54,495
[SPEAKER_00]: You don't get over there that quick.

383
00:35:55,096 --> 00:36:01,399
[SPEAKER_01]: I think I recall seeing him once in Cleveland on his hands and knees along the third-base line where he went into field, but he was going to roll foul, so he just let it go.

384
00:36:01,419 --> 00:36:04,161
[SPEAKER_01]: I think he even blew it.

385
00:36:04,301 --> 00:36:06,402
[SPEAKER_01]: I think I asked you once about your most difficult play.

386
00:36:06,422 --> 00:36:09,344
[SPEAKER_01]: Maybe that was that one-six or that double play that

387
00:36:12,172 --> 00:36:18,455
[SPEAKER_00]: If you're holding a man on first base, it's not too difficult because you don't have far to get back.

388
00:36:19,056 --> 00:36:38,547
[SPEAKER_00]: But if you're playing with a man on first and second and you're playing back fairly deep and you have to go to second and then to first, it's a little tougher because you have to move pretty quick after you've thrown the ball to second and you also have to look for the bag and then also look for the throw coming back at you.

389
00:36:39,173 --> 00:36:43,816
[SPEAKER_00]: And it's a little rougher than if you're holding just a man on first base.

390
00:36:44,937 --> 00:36:53,403
[SPEAKER_00]: I think one of the toughest plays for a first baseman is making up his mind whether to go for that slow hit ground ball or let the second baseman field it.

391
00:36:54,163 --> 00:37:01,929
[SPEAKER_00]: And you see this happen a lot because the first baseman is undecided whether to let it go or to break for it.

392
00:37:02,049 --> 00:37:08,333
[SPEAKER_00]: He'll break for a lot of balls that he shouldn't and you'll see the second baseman call him off and

393
00:37:08,913 --> 00:37:11,074
[SPEAKER_00]: And then the pitcher has to come over and cover the bag.

394
00:37:11,114 --> 00:37:16,095
[SPEAKER_00]: And he may be a little late getting there because he thought the first baseman was going to cover the first base anyway.

395
00:37:16,575 --> 00:37:21,897
[SPEAKER_00]: And this, I think, is a tough play for a first baseman, making up his mind to go for that ball or go for the bag.

396
00:37:23,257 --> 00:37:24,317
[SPEAKER_00]: What's your biggest thrill?

397
00:37:26,158 --> 00:37:30,819
[SPEAKER_00]: Well, my top thrill would have to be leading the league in hitting first time.

398
00:37:30,899 --> 00:37:36,981
[SPEAKER_00]: But I also have to rate an opening day thrill that

399
00:37:37,955 --> 00:37:43,198
[SPEAKER_00]: I had in 1954 when I hit a home run against the Yankees to win the game.

400
00:37:43,598 --> 00:37:46,780
[SPEAKER_00]: Allie Reynolds had come in to relieve.

401
00:37:48,261 --> 00:38:00,108
[SPEAKER_00]: After I circled the bases, a Secret Service man grabbed me and took me over to President Eisenhower's box to be congratulated by him, and the photographers were there.

402
00:38:00,268 --> 00:38:05,251
[SPEAKER_00]: After I had dressed, they rushed me out to Old Forest Park.

403
00:38:08,622 --> 00:38:19,065
[SPEAKER_00]: Not Forest Park, but oh, I can't even think of the hotel's name where Cameron Swayze was doing his news broadcast and I went on the show with him.

404
00:38:20,486 --> 00:38:21,686
[SPEAKER_00]: So this was an exciting day.

405
00:38:25,347 --> 00:38:32,189
[SPEAKER_01]: Well, Jay, I could go on here for some time, but how about one more, the toughest pitcher?

406
00:38:33,070 --> 00:38:33,750
[SPEAKER_00]: Bob Feller.

407
00:38:35,017 --> 00:38:38,982
[SPEAKER_00]: Bob Feller, without a doubt, he was the toughest for me.

408
00:38:39,662 --> 00:38:44,287
[SPEAKER_00]: I had a lot of tough ones, but if I have to pinpoint one, it would be Bob Feller.

